    "scienceEn": "Turkish coffee is the oldest documented method of coffee preparation (dating back to the Ottoman Empire in the 16th century). Beans are ground micro-fine (smaller than 70 microns, feeling like powder or cocoa). In a long-handled copper pot ('Cezve' or 'Ibrik'), grounds, cold water, and sugar are stirred and heated slowly (traditionally in a hot sand pan 'kumda kahve'). As water reaches ~90-95°C, expanding trapped gases push an unctuous, thick foam ('köpük') to the rim. It is immediately removed from heat before boiling. Served unfiltered; grounds settle to the bottom of the cup.",
